===Etymology===
From {{OE.}} ''[[smoc]]''; akin to {{OHG.}} ''[[smocho]]'', [[Icelandic]] ''[[smokkr]]'', and from the root of {{OE.}} ''[[smgan]]'' "to [[creep]]", akin to [[German]] ''[[schmiegen]]'' "to [[cling]] to", "[[press]] [[close]]". {{MHG.}} ''[[smiegen]]'', {{Icrl.}} ''[[smjga]]'' "to creep through", "to put on a garment which has a hole to put the head through"; compare with [[Lithuanian]] ''[[smukti]]'' "to [[glide]]". See also [[smug]], [[smuggle]].

===Pronunciation===
*Rhymes: [[Rhymes:English:-ɒk|-ɒk]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A woman's [[under-garment]]; a [[shift]]; a [[chemise]].
#:''In her smock, with head and foot all bare.'' Chaucer.
# A [[blouse]]; a [[smock frock]]. Carlyle.
# A loose [[garment]] worn as [[protection]] by a [[painter]] etc

